=== SUMMARY STATISTICS ===
Department sizes: HR=63, R&D=961, Sales=446
Attrition: HR=12, R&D=133, Sales=92
Attrition rates: HR=19.05%, R&D=13.84%, Sales=20.63%

=== KEY FACTORS CONTRIBUTING TO R&D LOWER ATTRITION ===

Factor 1: Job Role Composition
R&D has a diverse mix of roles including Research Directors (2.5% attrition),
Managers (5.6%), and Manufacturing Directors (6.8%) with very low attrition.
Sales has Sales Representatives (39.8% attrition) driving up the overall rate.

Factor 2: Within-Level Attrition
Across every job level, R&D has lower attrition than Sales.
Level 1: R&D 23.2% vs Sales 41.6%
Level 2: R&D 5.3% vs Sales 15.4%
Level 3: R&D 10.0% vs Sales 21.4%
Level 4: R&D 1.5% vs Sales 11.4%

Factor 3: Overtime Impact
Overtime employees in Sales have 38.0% attrition vs 27.2% in R&D
Non-overtime employees: Sales 13.7% vs R&D 8.5%

Factor 4: Stock Options
R&D employees more likely to have stock options (StockLevel=0: 42.4% vs 44.2% in Sales)
Within StockLevel=0: R&D 20.5% vs Sales 32.2%

Factor 5: Marital Status
Single employees: R&D 21.6% vs Sales 34.4%
R&D has fewer single employees (31.6% vs 34.2% in Sales)

Factor 6: Income within Level 1
R&D Level 1 avg income: $2,842 vs Sales Level 1: $2,513
